-- title:
--   Common knowledge relation of a Bayesian game
-- statement:
--   The **common knowledge relation** $E$ of a Bayesian game is the smallest equivalence relation on $\Omega$ containing every knowledge relation $E_i$ ($\omega\,E_i\,\omega'\iff t^i_\omega=t^i_{\omega'}$), i.e. the equivalence relation generated by $\bigcup_{i\in I}E_i$: $\omega\,E\,\omega'$ when $\omega$ and $\omega'$ are joined by a finite chain of states in which consecutive states are indistinguishable to some player.
--
--   The game is **hyperfinite** (resp. **smooth**) when $E$ is hyperfinite (resp. smooth) (Definitions 2.9 and 3.1).
-- source:
--   Hellman–Levy, Equilibria Existence in Bayesian Games: Climbing the Countable Borel Equivalence Relation Hierarchy, author's manuscript (2020-07-14), §2.6, p.10; Definition 2.9, p.11; Definition 3.1, p.12

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_HellmanLevy_Bayes_BayesianGame

namespace HellmanLevy.Bayes

/-- The **common knowledge relation** of a Bayesian game: the smallest equivalence relation
containing every knowledge relation `ω Eᵢ ω' ↔ tⁱ_ω = tⁱ_ω'`, i.e. the equivalence closure of
`⋃ᵢ Eᵢ`.
Hellman–Levy, *Equilibria Existence in Bayesian Games: Climbing the Countable Borel Equivalence
Relation Hierarchy*, author's manuscript (2020-07-14), §2.6, p.10 ("the transitive closure of the union ∪i∈IEi; in other words, it is the
minimal equivalence relation containing each element in E"); p.11 (Eᵢ from the types).

A Bayesian game is **hyperfinite** (resp. **smooth**), Definition 2.9 (p.11) and Definition 3.1
(p.12), when `CBER.IsHyperfinite (ckRel B)` (resp. `CBER.IsSmooth (ckRel B)`). -/
def ckRel {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] {I : Type*} {A : I → Type*}
    (B : BayesianGame Ω I A) : Ω → Ω → Prop :=
  Relation.EqvGen fun x y => ∃ i, B.t i x = B.t i y

end HellmanLevy.Bayes


